  "content": {
    "id": "p29-q10-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "10",
    "prompt": {
      "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
      "version": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "p29-q10-introduction",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 1,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Dirichlet’s Approximation Theorem provides information regarding how well a real number can be approximated by rational numbers. Of course an obvious way to obtain rational approximations to a real number is to consider its decimal representation. For example, ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "1.4,1.41,1.414,1.4142,1.41421,\\ldots",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " provide increasingly precise rational approximations to ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "\\sqrt{2}",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ". However, Dirichlet’s Approximation Theorem guarantees we can find rational approximations without the denominator growing too large. Its statement is as follows:",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "p29-q10-theorem-text",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 1,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Suppose ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "n\\in\\mathbb{N}",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " and ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "x\\in\\mathbb{R}",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ". Then there exists a fraction ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "\\frac{p}{q}",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " such that ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "1\\le q\\le n",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " and",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "p29-q10-theorem-bound",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "center",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 1,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "\\left|x-\\frac{p}{q}\\right|<\\frac{1}{nq}.",
              "display": true
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "p29-q10-instruction",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 1,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Prove the following consequence of this theorem:",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p29-q10a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "If $x$ is irrational, then there are infinitely many irreducible fractions $\\frac{p}{q}$ satisfying:\n$$\\left|x-\\frac{p}{q}\\right|<\\frac{1}{q^2}.$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "There are infinitely many such irreducible fractions.\n\nDirichlet’s theorem supplies approximations with positive errors tending to zero; finitely many rational numbers cannot do this.",
          "worked": "For each positive integer $n$, Dirichlet’s theorem gives $\\frac{p_n}{q_n}$ with $1\\le q_n\\le n$ and\n$$\\left|x-\\frac{p_n}{q_n}\\right|<\\frac{1}{nq_n}\\le\\frac{1}{q_n^2}.$$\nReduce this fraction to $\\frac{a_n}{b_n}$. Since $b_n\\le q_n$,\n$$\\left|x-\\frac{a_n}{b_n}\\right|<\\frac{1}{b_n^2}.$$\nAlso,\n$$0<\\left|x-\\frac{a_n}{b_n}\\right|<\\frac{1}{nq_n}\\le\\frac{1}{n},$$\nbecause $x$ is irrational. If only finitely many distinct reduced fractions occurred, their distances from $x$ would have a positive minimum $\\delta$. Choosing $n>\\frac{1}{\\delta}$ gives a distance less than $\\delta$, a contradiction. Therefore infinitely many distinct irreducible fractions satisfy the required inequality.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 88
      },
      {
        "id": "p29-q10b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p29-q10b-statement",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 1,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "If ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "x",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " is rational, then there are only finitely many irreducible fractions ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "\\frac{p}{q}",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " satisfying:",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "p29-q10b-bound",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "center",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 1,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "\\left|x-\\frac{p}{q}\\right|<\\frac{1}{q^2}.",
                  "display": true
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "p29-q10-visualisation-text",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 1,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "One way to visualise this result is to overlay circles onto the number line with centre ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "\\frac{p}{q}",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " and radius ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "\\frac{1}{q^2}",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": ", with there being one circle for each irreducible fraction ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "\\frac{p}{q}",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": ", as shown in the diagram below. A real number is irrational if and only if it is (strictly) contained within infinitely many of these circles.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [
          {
            "id": "p29-q10-diagram",
            "role": "question",
            "format": "image",
            "code": null,
            "src": "/booklet-assets/projects/enrichment-maths-1-part6-20261008/ae66834e567f-dirichlet-original-illustration.png",
            "widthMm": 110,
            "alt": "Mathematical diagram",
            "overlayOf": null,
            "transparent": false,
            "axes": null,
            "retentionReason": "Original illustration shows overlapping circles at rational centres p/q with radius1/q²; its finite cutoff and tiny-circle centres are not specified. Preserve exact isolated original pixels rather than inventing a plotted subset. Question, theorem formulas and proof remain fully editable.",
            "spec": {
              "sourcePage": 29,
              "sourceRegion": "Exact immutable original1406x1988 raster crop x698..1228,y1580..1778; full circles, ticks−2..2 and complete number-line arrows retained.",
              "sourceImageHash": "3eac27b81b8cd6c6b12e12d90a1931cb7781cff75edbfa3fd2ad45b5018d462b",
              "cropBounds": [
                698,
                1580,
                1228,
                1778
              ],
              "mathematics": "Original illustration of irreducible rational centres p/q and radius1/q², stated natively in the question. Preserve all original circles and overlap shading without inferring unspecified finite cutoff.",
              "answerVisibility": "Original supplied illustration only; no solution shown."
