        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "There are only finitely many such irreducible fractions.\n\nWriting $x=\\frac{P}{Q}$ in lowest terms bounds the possible denominators by $Q$ and the possible numerators by $Q+|P|$.",
          "worked": "Write $x=\\frac{P}{Q}$ in lowest terms, with $Q>0$, and take $\\frac{p}{q}$ in lowest terms with $q>0$. If $\\frac{p}{q}=\\frac{P}{Q}$, there is just one such irreducible fraction.\n\nOtherwise $qP-pQ$ is a non-zero integer. If $q>Q$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\left|\\frac{P}{Q}-\\frac{p}{q}\\right|&=\\frac{|qP-pQ|}{qQ}\\\\\\left|\\frac{P}{Q}-\\frac{p}{q}\\right|&\\ge\\frac{1}{qQ}\\\\\\left|\\frac{P}{Q}-\\frac{p}{q}\\right|&>\\frac{1}{q^2}.\\end{align*}$$\nThus every qualifying fraction has $q\\le Q$. If also $|p|\\ge Q+|P|$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\left|\\frac{P}{Q}-\\frac{p}{q}\\right|&\\ge\\frac{|p|}{q}-\\frac{|P|}{Q}\\\\\\left|\\frac{P}{Q}-\\frac{p}{q}\\right|&\\ge\\frac{|p|-|P|}{Q}\\\\\\left|\\frac{P}{Q}-\\frac{p}{q}\\right|&\\ge1\\\\\\left|\\frac{P}{Q}-\\frac{p}{q}\\right|&\\ge\\frac{1}{q^2}.\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore qualifying fractions satisfy $1\\le q\\le Q$ and $|p|<Q+|P|$. There are only finitely many integer pairs within these bounds, so only finitely many irreducible fractions satisfy the required inequality.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 124
      }
    ]
